The heat needed for a shower of volume V at temperature \theta_s, heated from the cold water temperature \theta_c, isQ_{shower} = \rho c_p V (\theta_s - \theta_c) = Q_{central} + Q_{unit} + Q_{rec}

where Q_{central} = \rho c_p V_{fresh} (\theta_s - \theta_c) is the heat in the fresh water from the central hot water system, Q_{unit} = P\,t the electric reheating in the unit at power P over the shower time t, and Q_{rec} the heat kept in the recirculated water.

The manufacturer states a water saving of 60 % for a 5-minute shower, 75 % for a 10-minute shower and 90 % for a 15-minute shower. In all three cases, the fresh water corresponds to about 2 minutes of a conventional shower. The fresh water is mostly the volume that fills the loop, and it depends little on the length of the shower.REVIEW LATER (Christian, 2026-10-15): check the CCE assumptions (price without installation, 0.68 DKK/SEK, n = 10 years). All data on the recirculating shower are from the manufacturer. Add the source of the 60/75/90 % water savings, and check the 2.5 kW reheating power, which is the largest uncertainty in the table.

Worked example.

A 30 m² room with a ceiling height of 2.5 m (75 m³) has one openable window on a single façade. The free opening area is A = 0.6 m² and the opening is H = 1.2 m tall. The wind speed is v = 2 m/s, the room is at 25 °C and the outdoor air at 20 °C, so \Delta T = 5 K. What is the airflow through the window?

Stack effect alone. With 

