Wind alone. With a single opening there is no pressure difference across the room, so 

(9) does not apply. The wind still drives some exchange through turbulence and pulsating flow at the opening. A simple empirical estimate 

Warren & Parkins, 1985 is Q_\text{wind} = 0.025 \, A \, v:Q_\text{wind} = 0.025 \cdot 0.6 \cdot 2 = 0.030 \text{ m}^3\text{/s} = 30 \text{ L/s}

Wind and stack combined. The two drivers do not simply add up across a single opening. EN 16798-7 uses an empirical expression for single-sided ventilation through an open window, based on measurements by de Gids and Phaff, where the larger of the two drivers decides:Q = 0.5 \, A \sqrt{\max\left(C_\text{wnd} \, v^2 \, ; \; C_\text{st} \, H \, \Delta T\right)}

with C_\text{wnd} = 0.001 (1/(m/s)) and C_\text{st} = 0.0035 ((m/s)/(m·K)). The wind term is 0.001 \cdot 2^2 = 0.004 and the stack term is 0.0035 \cdot 1.2 \cdot 5 = 0.021. The stack term is larger, soQ = 0.5 \cdot 0.6 \cdot \sqrt{0.021} = 0.044 \text{ m}^3\text{/s} = 44 \text{ L/s}

The models for single-sided ventilation differ considerably. Jiang et al. compared twelve of them with tracer-gas measurements on bottom-hung windows. Most models underestimated the airflow, and the EN 16798-7 model deviated by −82 % on average 

Jiang et al., 2025. The calculated airflows for single-sided ventilation are therefore uncertain and probably on the low side.

Result. The window gives about 45 to 60 L/s, or 2 to 3 air changes per hour. With 

